# Choosing what runs

Compilation and activation are different verbs.

A module can compile completely, including its dormant entry work, and still do nothing at runtime until an **entry** selects it. The seed can be fully formed and still sleep. Dependencies do not secretly run initialisers because someone mentioned a name.

Project entries are exactly the directories with an `@entry.moth` root. Presence is the only selection fact. An entry may be empty, static-only or metadata-only. `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` never become entries, and importing a module or package never activates its entry.

## Compile versus activate

Only an `@entry` root may carry dormant top-level runtime work and fragments. That work is parsed, type-checked, lowered and borrow-validated **before** any entry activates it. Local lifetime-region and escape analysis belongs to the same accepted sequence, but its implementation is still deferred.

The compiler synthesises an implicit **entry** for an `@entry` root. The entry has no source-level name, so `start` remains an ordinary identifier. Properties that matter:

- non-exported
- non-bindable
- built-in `Error!` alongside the existing fragment-collection success slots, including for a synthetic single-file root
- owner of dormant top-level runtime work and runtime fragment string production in source order

Compilation never activates the entry. Entry assembly may activate it once after the module already compiled.

Implicit checked failure materialises as `Error` in the entry. Typed `Error!` propagates with `!`; custom errors need explicit recovery. Builders consume that typed outcome without adding an error-fragment channel. The @../../../docs/progress (Progress matrix) records current support.

Support facades, the project package facade and `@mod` roots have no implicit entry.

In the walkthrough, `@entry.moth` owns dormant root work and a page fragment. `greeting.moth` is an ordinary source file inside that same directory-scoped module, not a separate module with a root of its own. Binding `greet` runs no entry statements, because only an activated entry root runs root work.

